  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The text in the small book, housed inside the oloid, is printed on strips of paper and mounted onto illustrations that resemble a cross-section of the human brain. The book opens into a circular shape.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">"The shape of the outer structure is a version of an oloid, a geometric shape discovered by sculptor and mathematician Paul Schatz in 1929"--Inside box lid.  The structure resembles a chrysalis.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">In a rectangular case with a display window. A key to the lettering printed inside the oloid is printed on a panel that the reader pulls from the base of the case.  The oloid, held closed with magnets, stands in the display case. A description of the work, and signature of the artist, are inside the lid.</subfield>
  </datafield>
